#* {{quote-book|author=Richard Sanders|chapter=What Physiognomie is, and what of It is Requisite for the Chiromancer to Know|title=Physiognomie, and Chiromancie, Metoposcopie, the Symmetrical Proportions and Signal Moles of the Body, Fully and Accurately Handled; with Their Natural-predictive Significations. The Subject of Dreams; Divinative, Steganographical, and Lullian Sciences. Whereunto is Added the Art of Memorie|location=London|publisher=Printed by R. White, for Nathaniel Brooke, at the sign of the angel in [[w:Cornhill, London|Cornhil]] near the [[w:Royal Exchange, London|Royal Exchange]]|year=1653|page=143|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hGxjAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA143|oclc=702365936|passage={{w|Hippocrates}} Prince of Phyſick, ſayes, that the Phyſician cannot be accompliſhed in his Art, if he have not the knowledge of ''Aſtrologie''; ſo I may ſay that the '''''Chiromancer''''' is not perfect nor accompliſhed if he have not ''Phyſiognomie'', which is ''Scienta quâ natura hominum ex aſpectu corporis judicatur'', {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Giovan[ni] Francesco Straparola|authorlink=Giovanni Straparola|author2=W. G. Waters|chapter=King Galafro's Vain Precautions|editor=Donald Beecher|title=[[w:The Facetious Nights of Straparola|The Pleasant Nights]]|series=The Lorenzo da Ponte Italian Library|location=Toronto|publisher={{w|University of Toronto Press}}|year=2012|volume=2|page=264|isbn=978-1-4426-4427-4|passage=Now this '''chiromancer''', when he understood the king's wish, took hold of his hand and examined with the greatest care all the lines he could trace there. After considering them diligently, one and all, he stood silent and his face grew pale.}}

